 {:seon.task/name "a-host-method-call-costs-a-dispatch"
  :seon.task/plan "Measured 2026-11-11 while cutting hasch: a method call on a host object costs about 190 ticks on Jolt (`.reset` on a `MessageDigest` alone 187; a SHA-512 of 2,368 bytes through libcrypto is 573), so a loop that calls one host method an element is dominated by dispatch (hasch's digest of 64 uuids: 19.3k of its ticks). A plain protocol method call on a deftype costs 10-13 ticks and allocates nothing (one type at the site), a call of five or more arguments 9 ticks and 80 B plus 16 B an argument (the same on upstream's head). So the 80-208 B a method call seen inside the sorted set, which the waived proof for `seon.db/eid` blames on a deftype method call's site cache (task jolt.backend/a-deftype-method-call-takes-a-site-cache), is more likely a host method call or a five-argument call: not yet measured there. Correct: name which call shape the sorted set's inner loop pays (a profile of 1,000 seeks on the Jolt at upstream's head), correct that waiver's stated cause with it, then cut the shape at its cause (a host site's lookup, or the argument list a wide call builds). Should cost: a host method call within a few times a protocol call."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-hashset-retainall-asks-its-argument"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: Jolt's ``java.util.HashSet`` answers ``retainAll`` by turning its argument into a list and, for each of its own elements, scanning that list with ``jolt=2`` (``reference-code/jolt/host/chez/java/host-static-classes.ss``, near line 1915): O(n x m), where the JDK asks the argument's own ``contains``, and where Jolt's own collection defaults already do (``coll-defaults.ss`` ``retainAll`` and ``removeAll`` by ``cd-has?``; HashSet's own ``removeAll`` and ``containsAll`` ask its hashtable). Measured 2026-11-11 (Deep fixes' helper, ``datahike.api/datoms-diff`` of two commits 203 scattered datoms apart on a 250k-datom LMDB store): 18.9 M of 22.2 M engine ticks in one ``retainAll`` of two HashSets of a few thousand UUIDs each, ~20 ticks a ``jolt=2``. Datahike's diff no longer calls it (fork commit e44bdfd4, Clojure sets); nothing else in the forks' or Seon's source calls ``retainAll``, ``removeAll`` or ``containsAll`` today, so this is a class left open in Jolt, not a cost Seon pays now. Correct: HashSet's ``retainAll`` asks ``(cd-has? c x)`` of its argument for each element, as ``coll-defaults.ss`` does (a hashed argument: one lookup an element), on a ``jolt/<topic>`` branch of the Jolt fork, offered upstream. Proof to write (named, not written): a Jolt unit case, `retainAll` of two HashSets of n elements at n = 1,000 and 10,000, whose engine ticks grow about 10x, not 100x, and whose answer is the JDK's."
